  "approximations": [
    {
      "what": "Schedule P Part I is not applied: every government pension is held to the 31,110 limit, and military pensions likewise.",
      "direction": "overstates_tax",
      "size": "4% of the excluded amount lost. A 60,000 federal pension with half its service before 1998 is fully excluded by Kentucky (30,000 under Part I, 30,000 under the limit); the projection excludes 31,110, so it overstates tax by 4% of 28,890 = 1,155.60 a year."
    },
    {
      "what": "One 3,270 standard deduction is applied to a married couple. On a combined return (Filing Status 2), open when both spouses had income, each spouse takes 3,270.",
      "direction": "overstates_tax",
      "size": "4% of 3,270 = 130.80 a year for a couple who both have income."
    },
    {
      "what": "The standard deduction is always taken; Kentucky itemized deductions (Schedule A) are not modelled.",
      "direction": "overstates_tax",
      "size": "4% of the amount by which itemized deductions exceed 3,270, for an itemizer."
    },
    {
      "what": "Interest on U.S. government bonds and securities is taxed (the projection cannot tell it from other interest).",
      "direction": "overstates_tax",
      "size": "4% of that interest."
    },
    {
      "what": "A projection that cannot tell Kentucky bonds from other states' bonds treats all tax-exempt interest as other-state and adds it back.",
      "direction": "overstates_tax",
      "size": "4% of the interest from Kentucky obligations."
    },
    {
      "what": "Kentucky's Internal Revenue Code date is December 31, 2024; the projection uses the household's current federal AGI and applies no other Schedule M additions or subtractions.",
      "direction": "either",
      "size": "Usually none for retirement income."
    },
    {
      "what": "Family size is taken from filing status: one for single, two for every other status; dependents are not modelled. On the form family size counts the filer, a spouse living in the same household and qualifying children (IRC 152(c)), up to four. A filer with qualifying children gets a smaller table here than on the form. A head of household or qualifying surviving spouse with no qualifying child, or a married separate filer who lived apart from the spouse all year, is family size one on the form and gets a larger table here.",
      "direction": "either",
      "size": "Only at or under 42,760 of modified gross income. Too much tax: up to the whole tax, e.g. 1,155.20 for a single parent of three with 32,150 of wages (family size four, 100%; family size one, none). Too little tax: up to the whole tax, e.g. 715.20 for a family-size-one head of household with 21,150 of wages (family size two, 100%)."
    },
    {
      "what": "Modified gross income is taken as federal AGI plus all tax-exempt interest. The form uses the greater of federal AGI plus non-Kentucky municipal interest and lump sums, or Kentucky AGI plus lump sums; Kentucky bond interest is included here and lump sums are left out.",
      "direction": "either",
      "size": "Each 10% band is 469 to 1,286 of income wide, so Kentucky bond interest (counted here, not on the form) lowers the credit by a band for about each such amount and can remove it (overstates tax); a lump sum left out of federal AGI (counted on the form, not here) does the opposite. None for a household with neither."
    },
    {
      "what": "The Family Size Tax Credit is a share of the tax after the personal tax credits (Form 740 line 19 after line 17), so a consumer must apply it after the 40 age credit.",
      "direction": "either",
      "size": "None when applied in that order. Applied to the tax before the age credit, the credit is too large by the share times the age credit: at most 0.9 x 80 = 72 a year for a couple both 65 or over (at 100% the tax is 0 either way)."
    },
    {
      "what": "The family size credit's income test uses the filer's own federal AGI on a married separate return. The Schedule ITC worksheet adds the spouse's federal AGI (line b) when the couple lives together.",
      "direction": "understates_tax",
      "size": "Only for a separate filer at or under 28,130, the end of the family size two column: up to the whole tax, at most 715.20 (own income 21,150, kept at 100% here, while the spouse's income would lift the couple past the table)."
    }
  ],
